 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566185 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566185 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186565941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566185 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ee637896-1be0-5705-861b-4b0376c0f76f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566195 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566195 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566195 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566195 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566195 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566195 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186051048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566195 #edb/ref 1089 #edb/ref 17592186566196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566195 #edb/ref 1090 #uuid "d6d905f9-2c23-5d31-aad4-69e916e7bfd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566196 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186566278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566196 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186566281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566196 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186566288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566196 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186566293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566196 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186566298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566196 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186566303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566196 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186566311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566196 #edb/ref 1085 "A company produces two types of products, type A and type B. The revenue function of the company, in thousands of dollars, is given by $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-1,\n$$\n\nwhere $x={[\\begin{aligned}\\,x & y\\,\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ $x$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product A, and $y$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product B. The company can adjust the prices per unit for both types of products by adding or removing certain features." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566196 #edb/ref 1086 #uuid "d9a60175-ea7e-5ff5-a696-1e759f0e8e78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566196 #edb/ref 1087 #edb/ref 17592186566278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566204 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566204 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566204 #edb/ref 1069 "q-168891" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566204 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566204 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the Hessian determinant of $f$ at $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(0,0).$\n\n$H(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)\\,=$ \n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,H(0,0)\\,=$ \n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566204 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "12e17457-b92e-5bbd-b375-4d92edbd2785" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566204 #edb/ref 1074 "We computed the second partial derivatives in part 5. So, the second derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}24{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}-16 & 32xy \\\\ 32xy & 16{x}^{2}+12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n* Evaluating the second derivative at $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)=[\\begin{aligned}32 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 44\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nH(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)=32\\cdot 44-0\\cdot 0=1\\,408.\n$$\n\n* Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=-16\\cdot 12-0\\cdot 0=-192.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566206 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566206 #edb/ref 1069 "q-168888" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566206 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566206 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1d/1d2132be4de3f505e5052ae3083b75ac8aceceed9f64bf62d128746a37a59a23.png)\n\nGiven a sketch of the graph of $z=f(x)$ above, which of the points ($A,$ $B,$ or $C$) is a local minimum of the function?\n\nA local minimum is the point \n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566206 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "187224fe-1282-5974-b5d9-f692bdbb4202" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566206 #edb/ref 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nAmong the given options, such neighborhoods exist for the point $C.$ Hence, $C$ is a local minimum.\n\n![](images/a3/a389560d6217e86b82384443c5730168483a75a1759788ddde31ef64ad368adc.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566217 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566217 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566217 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566217 #edb/ref 1069 "q-168889" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566217 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566217 #edb/ref 1071 "The critical points of $f$ are$\\,$$(\\pm $ \n{{field-1}}\n$,$ \n{{field-2}}\n$)$ and $(0,$ \n{{field-3}}\n$).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566217 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ed0a30c-70d1-57fa-8ba9-ce180a14b5a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566217 #edb/ref 1074 "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x & \\,16{x}^{2}y+12y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x=0 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}y+12y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}8x({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2)=0 \\\\ 4y(4{x}^{2}+3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $4{x}^{2}+3\\ne 0,$ from the second equation of the system, we obtain that\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ 8x({x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ 8x({x}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(0,0).$\n\nNote that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these points, they are also stationary points." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566225 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566225 #edb/ref 1069 "q-168886" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566225 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566225 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,=${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566225 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d7ebf34-a035-5c2e-ad76-841acaa3c221" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566225 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{4})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{4})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the constant multiple rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{4})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2}) & =2\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4})+8{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})-8\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4{x}^{3}+8{y}^{2}\\cdot 2x-8\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566244 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566244 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566244 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566244 #edb/ref 1069 "q-168892" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566244 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566244 #edb/ref 1071 "Classify the critical points using the second partial derivatives test.\n\n$(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ are local minima since $\\,H(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$\n{{field-1}}\n$0\\,\\,$ and $\\,\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$\n{{field-2}}\n$0$\n\n$(0,0)$ is a saddle point since $\\,\\,H(0,0)$\n{{field-3}}\n$0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566244 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75a986c0-75fe-5567-a170-fed953f3d35d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566244 #edb/ref 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n* If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n  * if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$ \n\n  * if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n* If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n* If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nIn part 4, we found the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(0,0).$ Their corresponding Hessian determinants and the second partial derivatives at these points were computed in part 6.\n\nWith that in mind, let's classify our critical points:\n\n* $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ are local minima of $f$ since $H(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)=1\\,408>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)=32>0.$\n\n* $(0,0)$ is a saddle point since $H(0,0)=-192<0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566263 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566263 #edb/ref 1069 "q-168887" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566263 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566263 #edb/ref 1071 "${f}^{′}(x)=$$[\\,8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x$ \n{{field-1}}\n$]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566263 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7c3402d-fbe9-5875-8d06-b63e54fe220e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566263 #edb/ref 1074 "For our scalar function, we have that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={(∇f)}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEarlier, we found that \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x.\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-1)=16{x}^{2}y+12y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x\\, & \\,16{x}^{2}y+12y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566270 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566270 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566270 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186566174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566270 #edb/ref 1069 "q-168890" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566270 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566270 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the second partial derivatives of $f.$\n\n$\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\,=$\n{{field-2}}\n\n$\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}=\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}\\,=$\n{{field-3}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566270 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ed3eb4a9-71c7-55f2-a5c3-dcaf4e7245dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566270 #edb/ref 1074 "Previously, we found that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=16{x}^{2}y+12y.\n$$\n\nComputing the second partial derivatives, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x)=24{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}-16 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(16{x}^{2}y+12y)=32xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(16{x}^{2}y+12y)=16{x}^{2}+12\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566278 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186566288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566278 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0f155874-d644-5c6d-a1bc-950a7f264867" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566278 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186566225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566281 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186566298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566281 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "11cf8195-97e7-51c0-b62c-f526c31dc712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566281 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186566270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566288 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186566293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566288 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f476aaa-d1ae-51b7-98e7-b3b01846bd0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566288 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186566263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566293 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186566311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566293 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "58d0d71f-5f0f-5b59-82c6-8d354187fa5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566293 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186566206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566298 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186566303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566298 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "90fd6369-1814-5dfa-84f9-2c77827013fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186566298 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 1759